From 0f282122cdbf23803bdc42c0556a066b3e1f1f4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:13:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] estuary: an overview page in the player information, fed from
 the real output state
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Kodi's player information is split into short pages that each show a few
lines; what a viewer of a film wants to know at once (what the display is
really sent, what the audio output is really sent, what Dolby Vision is
doing and why) is spread over them or not there at all. The page that
opens first for a video is now one full-height overview in three columns:

- Video and audio of the source: format, codec, picture size, scan and
  frame rate, aspect, pixel format, decoder, live bitrate; audio codec
  (with Atmos / DTS:X as the demuxer names it), channels and layout,
  sample format, decoder, what the audio output is sent and how, its
  signal, bitrate, language, subtitles.
- Dolby Vision: output state and the reason when it is not native,
  profile, layers, FEL residual, frames shown in the last 10 s, longest
  gap, L1 changes; the RPU metadata of the frame shown: source display,
  L1 in nits and as PQ codes, L2 trims with their target displays, L5 of
  the RPU and L5 sent, L6, the blocks of the RPU, those sent and those not
  sent. For HDR10 output (also Dolby Vision sent as HDR10), the static
  metadata that the display is sent: mastering luminance, primaries,
  MaxCLL / MaxFALL.
- Video output: display model (EDID), connector and DRM driver, mode,
  the signal (native Dolby Vision, HDR10, HLG, SDR), colorimetry, the
  transport of native Dolby Vision or the framebuffer format, and the
  output depth as the driver treats it; processing and system: the timing
  of native Dolby Vision frames from the Dolby Vision page's properties
  and strings (GPU time and headroom over 10 s, how frames without GUI
  elements are drawn, frame ahead, the fidelity step-down in words), CPU
  and its temperature, memory, player cache, video and audio queues.

Nothing on the page is estimated: a value that is not known says so, and
a row that does not apply is not shown. The new values come from the
places that hold them:

- the connector's Colorspace, max bpc and HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA as the
  kernel took them with the last commit it accepted (CDRMAtomic caches
  them then; asking the kernel would take all its mode-setting locks from
  the thread that draws), and the content of that blob;
- the output depth: no driver reports the bits per colour on the link.
  On amdgpu, i915 and xe "max bpc" is an upper limit ("up to N bits; the
  driver chooses"). On nvidia-drm, Hades' own property drives the output
  at exactly 8 below 10 (native Dolby Vision) and otherwise leaves it to
  NVKMS, at most nvidia-modeset's max_output_color_depth (10 unless set;
  8 on HDMI without hdmi_deepcolor), read once from sysfs: the default 12
  of the property is not what is sent;
- the plane scanned out (or that the video has a plane of its own), the
  mode set, the EDID's model name (CDisplayInfo::GetModel()), the DRM
  driver's name;
- the audio engine's sink format: a bitstream has its kind there (the
  source's own, or Kodi's AC-3 / E-AC-3 encoding, told apart in the skin
  by the source codec), PCM its channels, rate and sample format;
- the numbers of the stream (sample rate, bits, live bitrates), the use
  of all processors (System.CpuUsage gives each one on Linux) and the
  memory: computed as Kodi's own labels compute them, but without the
  thousands separator that StringUtils::FormatNumber() takes from the
  region. Kodi writes decimals with a point in every language (fps,
  aspect, Mb/s); next to them a "48.000" or "5,421" could be read as a
  decimal. No number on the page has a thousands separator, and its
  decimals are points, as on Kodi's other pages.

They are published as hades.out.*, hades.audio.*, hades.video.* and
hades.sys.* properties of the Home window, next to hades.dv.*, by
PublishPlayerInfo(): only while the dialog is visible, at most twice a
second, and all cleared when it closes so that it never opens on what
was true earlier. CDRMObject::GetPropertyEnumName() names an enum value.

Kodi's pages stay as they were, a press away on the same row of buttons,
which the overview carries in its header; Dolby Vision video no longer
opens on the Dolby Vision page, whose content the overview has. The rows
are includes of their own (Includes_HadesPlayerInfo.xml): a row, a wide
row for values that need the column, a heading, a note. Every text of
the page is a string of the skin's own files, in English and in the three
Spanish ones, es_es writing "vídeo" and es_ar / es_mx "video" (Kodi's own
strings may be untranslated or spelt otherwise there; only Yes and No of
the Dolby Vision page's HadesDVAheadVar are Kodi's): strings 31800 to
31869, and es_ar / es_mx of Hades' 31656, 31659 and 31677, which wrote
"vídeo" as es_es does.

Checked without a display, since Kodi's GUI does not start without a
graphics driver: every XML well formed; every string, variable, include
and property that the page uses defined, its strings translated and
spelt for each locale (the check fails on ten mutations); the page
evaluated for seven cases in the four languages, with every text measured
in the skin's font against the room it has, every column against its
height, no comma between digits and one spelling per locale, and every
value of each variable (the evaluation fails on four mutations); and the
helpers that format the values run on the host.
